Let $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a compact immersed surface with non-empty boundary, satisfying the following properties:

(i) $\Sigma$ has constant mean curvature;
(ii) the boundary $\partial\Sigma$ is a round circle in some plane of $\mathbb{R}^3$;
(iii) $\Sigma$ is star-shaped with respect to some point $p \in \mathbb{R}^3$, meaning that every ray emanating from $p$ meets $\Sigma$ in at most one point.

Recall that a spherical cap is the (closed) intersection of a round 2-sphere in $\mathbb{R}^3$ with a closed half-space whose bounding plane meets the sphere transversally, and a planar disk is the closed flat disk bounded by a circle in a plane.

Prove or disprove: under hypotheses (i)–(iii), $\Sigma$ must be either the flat planar disk bounded by $\partial\Sigma$ or a spherical cap with boundary $\partial\Sigma$.

